SwirlOS philosophy
==================

1. EVERYTHING IS A REAL FILE ON A REAL DISK.

   Every resource SwirlOS uses lives in SwirlFS, at a real path, traversable
   by the file manager and by any user program:

     /system/fonts/*.ttf    Real TrueType fonts. The kernel parses and
                            rasterizes them at RUNTIME. No font pixels are
                            baked into the kernel binary.
     /system/apps/*.elf     All applications, including the shell, are
                            ordinary ELF64 executables on disk.
     /system/config/*.conf  Kernel and shell configuration, read at boot.
     /system/lib/           The userspace runtime (libswirl.a) apps link to.
     /system/doc/           System documentation (you are reading it).
     /system/kernel/        A visible copy of the boot kernel image.
     /home/user/            User files. Shell-created files persist on disk.

   The only exceptions are low-level boot artifacts: GRUB and its modules
   live on the boot ISO (an ordinary ISO9660 filesystem), and the kernel
   keeps a tiny 8x8 emergency font for panic screens before the disk
   driver is up. That emergency font is never used for normal operation.

2. RINGS ARE REAL.

   The kernel executes at CPL 0 (ring 0). Every application, including the
   shell, executes at CPL 3 (ring 3). The CPU enforces the boundary: ring-3
   code cannot touch kernel memory or hardware; it can only ask, via the
   int 0x80 syscall interface. Ring 1 and ring 2 segments are defined in
   the GDT but intentionally unused (reserved for future servers).

3. SWIRLOS IS NOT LINUX.

   - No fork(). Processes are created with spawn(), which loads an ELF
     file directly into a fresh process. One syscall, no copy-on-write.
   - One unified /system tree instead of /usr, /etc, /bin, /sbin sprawl.
   - SwirlFS instead of ext4: simple, deterministic, fully specified in
     /system/doc/swirlfs-spec.txt.
   - The syscall ABI is SwirlOS's own (see /system/doc/syscall-abi.txt).
   - The kernel does not hide its filesystem: there is no initramfs and
     no opaque blob. What you see in the file manager is the disk.
